actory-init\n39:Then build and install the dispatch supervisor:\n42:cd ~/.darkfactory/tools/darkfactory-dispatch && make install\n47:Symlinks mean the skills always point to your local clone, so updating is just a `git pull` (see below).\n49:Restart Claude Code, then from any project run:\n55:This checks your entire setup and tells you exactly what's missing. It verifies core skills, supporting skills (including `/test-quality-review` for L [... omitted end of long line]\n59:Since skills are symlinked to your clone, pulling updates everything:\n71:The orchestrator is the main Claude Code session that runs your mission. It starts by interviewing you about what you want to build, asking pointed qu [... omitted end of long line]\n90:For each task, the orchestrator sends a worker (Codex or Claude, your choice) a brief describing what to build. The worker writes the code, commits it [... omitted end of long line]\n117:3. **Independent code review** -- two separate reviewers (Codex and Claude, running at the same time) each review all the changes against the full con [... omitted end of long line]\n129:**Orchestrator** -- the main Claude Code session you interact with. It plans the work, sends out one worker at a time, reads their results, runs valid [... omitted end of long line]\n131:**Workers** -- each worker builds a single task. By default, workers are powered by Codex (OpenAI's coding agent), but you can use Claude instead. Cod [... omitted end of long line]\n151:All mission state lives in files on disk, not in memory. This has two practical benefits: if your Claude Code session crashes or you close the termina [... omitted end of long line]\n171:## Skills\n173:Darkfactory is controlled through skills -- reusable prompt templates you invoke with a slash command in Claude Code.\n175:| Skill | Purpose |\n180:| `/darkfactory-init` | Check your project setup. Reports what's installed, what's missing, and gives you the exact commands to fix it. |\n182:### Skill structure